{
  "rules": [
    "Strong negative level bias: forecasts sit ~3–6 EUR/t below realized prices across the whole 30d horizon (persistent underprediction in rising regime). Apply an upward intercept shift when recent trend is up.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: model captures direction but reacts too slowly to rallies; realized series accelerates higher while forecast stays near recent mean. Add a momentum term proportional to last 5–10d slope (and/or 2nd derivative) to lift the path.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: error grows with lead time (later days are more underpredicted than early days). Use a lead-time scaling so the upward correction increases with horizon (e.g., linear ramp).",
    "Excess mean-reversion: forecasts repeatedly pull back toward ~63–66 even when market breaks out to ~68–70. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above its 20–30d moving average and MA slope is positive (trend regime filter).",
    "Breakout miss after local lows: following a dip (around ~60–62 in history), model expects continued softness but market rebounds sharply. If last price is below MA by >~1–2 EUR and momentum turns positive, bias correction should flip upward (rebound heuristic)."
  ]
}
